'''Grup Tècnic Petrolí, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Grup Tècnic Petrolí}}), commonly referred to as '''GTP''', is a Balboan {{w|oilfield services}} company and the largest provider of construction, maintenance, and supply services to the petroleum industry in [[Abyala]]. The company designs, builds, and maintains {{w|oil refinery|refineries}}, {{w|oil platform|offshore rigs}}, {{w|pipeline transport|pipelines}}, and associated infrastructure, and supplies parts and materials to oil and gas operators across the region. It is headquartered in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]] and operates in [[Balboa]], [[Sanrobonia]], [[Potocsí]], [[Balisca]], and [[Tapuya]].

GTP is dual-listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: GTP) and the [[Sanropura Stock Exchange]] (SRX: GTP), reflecting its significant operations in Sanrobonia and the cross-border nature of the Abyalan petroleum industry. It is a constituent of the [[SCX 30]] index. [[CIEA]], one of the world's largest oil companies by revenue and GTP's largest single client, holds a 10% strategic stake in the company.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1968–1979) ===

[[Carles Bonet i Riera]] founded GTP in 1968. Bonet was a construction engineer who had worked in the construction division of [[Petrolí de Balboa]] (PB), the state-owned oil company, during the 1960s expansion of the [[Badia Curta]] refinery on the [[Shendan]] coast in [[Jala]] province. During this period, PB relied heavily on foreign contractors for specialised refinery construction and maintenance work, and Bonet identified an opportunity to build a domestic Balboan company capable of providing these services.

GTP was initially a small construction contractor, providing civil works and structural steel erection for PB's refinery expansion. The company's competitive advantage was its familiarity with PB's engineering standards and its ability to work within the constraints of the Balboan construction sector — limited heavy-lift capacity, a shortage of specialised welders, and the logistical challenges of the Shendan coast. Bonet recruited experienced construction workers and engineers from the broader Balboan industrial sector and invested in training programmes for refinery-specific skills.

By the mid-1970s, GTP had expanded its services to include mechanical maintenance for PB's refining infrastructure, including {{w|turnaround (oil refinery)|turnaround}} and shutdown services — the periodic planned maintenance shutdowns that refineries undergo to inspect, repair, and replace equipment. This was a significant step, as turnaround work required a larger workforce, specialised equipment, and the ability to mobilise quickly. GTP's domestic workforce and proximity to Badia Curta gave it a cost and logistical advantage over foreign contractors.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1979, raising capital to expand its workforce and equipment fleet. The Bonet family retained a controlling stake.

=== Expansion across Abyala (1980s–1990s) ===

The 1980s saw GTP expand beyond Balboa for the first time. The company's initial international expansion was driven by the growth of the Sanrobonian oil industry, which was developing offshore fields in the Sanrobonian continental shelf. GTP established a Sanrobonian subsidiary in 1983, headquartered in [[Sanropura]], to provide offshore rig construction and maintenance services to the Sanrobonian oil companies operating the fields. The expansion was facilitated by the close relationship between the Balboan and Sanrobonian petroleum industries — PB (later CIEA) had long-standing supply relationships with Sanrobonian oil producers, and GTP's reputation as a reliable refinery contractor opened doors in the Sanrobonian offshore market.

In the late 1980s, GTP expanded its parts and materials supply business, establishing a dedicated supply chain division. The company began sourcing, stocking, and distributing specialised oilfield equipment — {{w|valve|valves}}, {{w|pipe (material)|pipes}}, {{w|pump|pumps}}, {{w|heat exchanger|heat exchangers}}, {{w|gasket|gaskets}}, and {{w|flange|flanges}} — for oil and gas operators across its markets. This business was driven by the recognition that oil companies operating in Abyala faced long lead times for parts imported from Illypnian manufacturers, and that a regional distributor with local warehousing could significantly reduce downtime for maintenance and repairs.

CIEA's 1996 merger with [[Syarikat Minyak Sanrobonian Selatan]] (SMSS) created a pan-Abyalan oil major with operations spanning Balboa, Sanrobonia, and Potocsí, and GTP's existing presence in both the Balboan and Sanrobonian markets positioned it as a natural contractor for the merged company's infrastructure projects. In 1998, CIEA acquired a 10% strategic stake in GTP, formalising the relationship and ensuring GTP's alignment with CIEA's long-term infrastructure plans. The same year, GTP dual-listed on the [[Sanropura Stock Exchange]], giving Sanrobonian investors access to the company and reflecting the growing importance of Sanrobonian operations to GTP's revenue.

The 1990s also saw GTP expand into Potocsí, where the development of the [[Marañón basin]] oil fields created demand for drilling support services and pipeline construction. GTP established a Potocsino subsidiary in 1995, headquartered in [[Port Soledat]], and began providing construction and maintenance services for [[PETROPOTO]] (Potocsino Petroleum), the state-owned oil company, as well as other operators in the basin.

=== Recent developments (2000s–2020s) ===

The 2000s and 2010s saw GTP consolidate its position as the largest oil services company in Abyala. The company expanded into [[Balisca]] and [[Tapuya]], providing construction and maintenance services for the oil and gas industries in both countries. GTP's geographic diversification reduced its dependence on any single market and gave it a competitive advantage over international oil services companies, which typically lacked GTP's depth of local knowledge and relationships.

In the 2020s, GTP has faced the dual challenge of the global energy transition and the maturation of the Abyalan oil industry. The company has begun to diversify into {{w|renewable energy}} infrastructure, providing construction services for {{w|solar farm|solar farms}} and {{w|wind farm|wind farms}} in Balboa and Sanrobonia, and has invested in {{w|carbon capture and storage|carbon capture}} technology for refinery applications. However, the core oil and gas services business remains the dominant source of revenue, driven by the continued expansion of the Sanrobonian and Potocsino oil industries and the ongoing maintenance needs of CIEA's Badia Curta refinery complex.

== Operations ==

GTP's operations are organised into four business divisions:

* '''Refinery construction and maintenance''': The company's original and largest business, providing {{w|EPC (engineering, procurement and construction)|EPC}} contracting for new refinery construction, turnaround and shutdown services, and ongoing mechanical maintenance. GTP is the primary maintenance contractor for [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery complex, the largest refinery in Abyala, and has built or maintained refineries for oil companies across the region.
* '''Offshore rig construction and maintenance''': GTP constructs, installs, and maintains offshore drilling rigs and production platforms for oil companies operating in Sanrobonian and Potocsino waters. The company's Sanrobonian subsidiary operates a fleet of offshore construction vessels and maintains a yard in Sanropura for rig fabrication and repair.
* '''Parts and materials supply''': GTP sources, stocks, and distributes specialised oilfield equipment — {{w|valve|valves}}, {{w|pipe (material)|pipes}}, {{w|pump|pumps}}, {{w|heat exchanger|heat exchangers}}, {{w|gasket|gaskets}}, {{w|flange|flanges}}, and other components — through a network of warehouses across Abyala. The supply business reduces lead times for oil companies and provides a steady revenue stream that is less cyclical than construction.
* '''Engineering and technical consulting''': GTP provides feasibility studies, {{w|front-end engineering design}} (FEED), project management, and technical consulting for oil and gas infrastructure projects. This division serves both GTP's internal construction projects and external clients, and has been a growing source of higher-margin revenue.

== Geographic presence ==

GTP operates across five Abyalan countries, with its revenue distributed approximately as follows:

* '''[[Balboa]]''' (~30%): Headquarters in [[Sant Cristòfor]]; primary operations at [[Badia Curta]] ([[Jala]] province), where GTP maintains the CIEA refinery complex; additional operations at Portnou and Sant Bart for domestic oil infrastructure
* '''[[Sanrobonia]]''' (~25%): Subsidiary headquartered in [[Sanropura]]; offshore rig construction and maintenance; parts warehousing; the company's SRX listing
* '''[[Potocsí]]''' (~20%): Subsidiary headquartered in [[Port Soledat]]; drilling support and pipeline construction for the [[Marañón basin]] oil industry; major client is [[PETROPOTO]]
* '''[[Balisca]]''' (~15%): Construction and maintenance services for Baliscan oil and gas infrastructure
* '''[[Tapuya]]''' (~10%): Construction and maintenance services for the Tapuyan energy sector

The company's geographic diversification is a key competitive advantage, as it allows GTP to offset cyclical downturns in individual markets and to mobilise resources across borders for large projects. The dual-listing on SCX and SRX reflects the importance of both the Balboan and Sanrobonian markets to the company's operations.

== Relationship with CIEA ==

GTP's relationship with [[CIEA]] is the cornerstone of its business. CIEA is GTP's largest single client, accounting for an estimated 35–40% of total revenue, and the relationship spans the full range of GTP's services: refinery construction and maintenance at [[Badia Curta]], offshore rig maintenance for CIEA's Sanrobonian operations, parts supply across CIEA's Abyalan footprint, and engineering consulting for CIEA's infrastructure projects.

CIEA's 10% strategic stake in GTP, acquired in 1998, formalises the relationship and ensures alignment between the two companies' long-term plans. The stake gives CIEA a seat on GTP's board and a degree of influence over the company's strategic direction, while allowing GTP to remain an independent, publicly traded company that serves multiple clients.

Beyond CIEA, GTP's second-largest client is [[PETROPOTO]] (Potocsino Petroleum), the state-owned oil company of [[Potocsí]]. GTP provides drilling support, pipeline construction, and maintenance services for PETROPOTO's operations in the [[Marañón basin]], and the relationship has grown significantly as Potocsino oil production has expanded. Other major clients include the state oil companies of [[Balisca]] and [[Tapuya]], as well as independent operators across the region.

The relationship with CIEA is not without tension. GTP's dependence on CIEA for a significant portion of its revenue makes it vulnerable to changes in CIEA's capital spending, and the company has sought to diversify its client base by expanding its services to PETROPOTO and other oil companies operating in Abyala. However, CIEA's dominant position in the Abyalan oil industry — and the strategic importance of the Badia Curta refinery complex — ensures that the relationship will remain central to GTP's business for the foreseeable future.

== Ownership ==

GTP's ownership structure reflects its origins as a family-founded business, its strategic relationship with CIEA, and its status as a publicly traded company with significant institutional shareholders:

* [[Bonet family]]: 18% — the founding family, descended from [[Carles Bonet i Riera]]; [[Carles Bonet i Masdeu]], the founder's grandson, serves as Chairman
* [[CIEA]]: 10% — strategic stake acquired in 1998
* [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]]: 8%
* [[Balboa Pension Fund]]: 5%
* Public float: ~59%

The company is professionally managed, with the CEO position held by [[Rosa Vives i Carbonell]], an external hire with extensive experience in the international oil services industry. The Bonet family retains board representation through the Chairmanship but does not hold executive management positions.

